				\begin{document}$$BiTCN_{output} = TCN_{forward} \left( x \right) + TCN_{backward} \left( x \right)$$\end{document}

The outputs generated by the BiTCN modules are subsequently fed into Capsule Networks, processed independently for each feature stream. Unlike traditional CNNs that rely on scalar neurons, Capsule Networks utilize vector-based capsules to capture spatial hierarchies and inter-feature dependencies. This structure is architecturally designed to model hierarchical relationships among learned sequence features, which can enhance the representation of complex dependencies in protein and peptide sequences, as reflected by improved predictive performance in this study50. A core component of Capsule Networks is the dynamic routing algorithm, which ensures that only the most relevant and informative features are passed from one capsule layer to the next. This is mathematically expressed as:24\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
